A||||9||skitime||Front V6 manifold heat sheild? Why?||||5||
Z||000000||skitime||02-03-2001||12:40 PM||||I have the engine out and am ready to put the manifolds on and am scratching my head as to what the heat shiild is shielding. The starter? The dipstick? That's all that is there. Wouldn't it be better to get the shield out from the manifold and head to reduce the chance of leaks. The shield could be mounted in the same position without being between the manifold and head.  Wouldn't that be better? I know the shield was put there after a recall but it seems to be a poor design.  Your thoughts please!  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||02-03-2001||01:16 PM||||I imagine it deflects some heat away from the valve cover but the main job is to keep a leaking valve cover from dumping oil on the exhaust. Leave it where it is between the manifold and head. <P>The thing may not leak now but since it's hard to notice it if it starts leaking later this gives you some extra insurance to prevent a fire.||||reg||1
Z||000002||skitime||02-03-2001||01:50 PM||||If it keeps a leaky valve cover gasket from leaking oil on the manifold then why wouldn't they put one on the back manifold as well?  Why just the front? Why not put a sheild at the valve cover?||||reg||1
Z||000003||skitime||02-04-2001||04:43 PM||||Is anyone running their V6 without the heat shield under the manifold?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||02-04-2001||05:59 PM||||You did port your manifolds while they were off didn't you?||||reg||1
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||02-05-2001||07:00 AM||||It seemed that that heat shield that just goes down the side of the block was an over reaction to the engine fires. Perhaps the idea was to keep the oil away from the exhaust. The other heat shield that goes under the manifold makes more sense... I took mine off because the oil was just coating the shield from the valve covers and I didn't even want to find out what would happen if it were to go up!<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 02-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||GT Bastard||02-05-2001||07:48 AM||||Just ditch it.... You'll probably notice all the carbon build-up on the heat shield around the space where the exhaust flanges were.  I think it contributes to exhaust leaks.||||reg||1
Z||000007||skitime||02-05-2001||08:29 AM||||Yes I did port the manifolds.  Its unbelievable how poorly they were made.  I feel that if the shield was so important then they would of put one on the rear manifold as well.  I did see black carbon around the shield and on the heads which meant there were leaks around the shield. In addition is seems s stupid to to put the shield in without gaskets on both sides.  I feel I will leave the shield out.    ||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||02-05-2001||10:45 AM||||the only shields i left on my car were the one to protect the coil from turbo heat and the one on back of the alternator which burns out every couple years anyway. I took out all the grubby insulation too. I can tell pretty fast if i got any leaks because i keep it in the garage, all i have to do is look on the floor.||||reg||1
Z||000009||FIEROGOD||02-07-2001||09:51 PM|||| The only important heat sheild that i put back on all the 2.8s is the one that wraps around the front manifold. I have done several engine swaps and fixed many exhaust leaks. I have found that ditching the sheilds make the car run cooler. The sheild between the maniflod and the block i think just causes problems. I have put alot of miles mostly highway miles on my 86SE and have not had a problem.||||reg||1
